-#### Gradient Shading for Turbulent Flow Visualization
-**Definition:** A technique to obtain crisp and solid-looking images from volume-rendered turbulent flow data by applying additional gradient shading, solving the problem of 'cloud like or fuzzy' images characteristic of direct volume rendering of space filling 3-D data [S1].
-**Step:** Apply solid-texture-mapping to map one velocity component (e.g., vertical component) onto another (e.g., streamwise velocity component). Apply gradient shading to the texture-mapped volume data. Add lighting contributions including ambient, diffuse, specular, and glossiness to the image [S1].
-**Parameter:** Data source: Turbulent boundary layer over a flat plate simulation with 9.4 million grid points for 104 time steps, containing three velocity components, three vorticity components, and pressure. Hardware: Stardent GS-1000 [S1].
-**Number:** The resulting turbulence database contains 54 gigabytes of data [S1].
-**Comparison:** Unlike geometrical rendering techniques, this representation preserves the full integrity of the original dataset, making it more adaptable to analysis and extraction of quantitative information [S1].
+This method approximates directional shadows and light scattering in volume rendering by computing the extinction of light intensity for voxels using a 3D Summed Area Table (SAT). It solves the problem of efficiently calculating soft shadows and ambient occlusion (AO) without propagating illumination from outside the volume or requiring expensive per-sample shadow rays. Unlike [Direct Volume Rendering with Phong Shading](3686-direct-volume-rendering-with-phong-shading.md) which relies on local gradients, or [Illumination Caching](7548-illumination-caching.md) which may use different pre-computation strategies, this technique specifically models the attenuation of light through the volume by covering the light cone with axis-aligned cuboids. It is distinct from [Kniss et al.'s half-angle slicing](https://www.ifi.uzh.ch/dam/jcr:ffffffff-9e7f-2af5-ffff-ffffcf9259bb/FastShadows.pdf) by handling any light type and multiple sources, and from [Ropinski et al.'s method](https://www.ifi.uzh.ch/dam/jcr:ffffffff-9e7f-2af5-ffff-ffffcf9259bb/FastShadows.pdf) by computing extinction rather than propagating external illumination.
 
 ## Background
-The application of lighting models to volume rendering originated in the mid-1990s to address the lack of depth cues in direct volume rendering of semi-transparent data. Early work by Westover and Levoy introduced the concept of using gradients to estimate surface normals for Phong shading [S1, S4, unofficial]. Subsequent developments focused on optimizing these calculations, leading to techniques like illumination caching [S1, S7548, unofficial] and extinction-based shading using Summed Area Tables [S1, S9147, S9149, unofficial] to handle complex lighting effects like shadows and ambient occlusion efficiently. The integration of these shading techniques with ray casting remains a fundamental aspect of high-fidelity volume visualization [S1, S4, S8307, unofficial].
+The need for lighting in volume rendering arises from the fact that direct volume rendering of space-filling 3D data, such as fluid flow or medical scans, characteristically results in "cloud-like or fuzzy" images lacking depth cues [S1]. Early work by Raskar et al. demonstrated that applying gradient shading to volume-rendered turbulent flow data could produce crisp, solid-looking images, revealing coherent structures like hairpin vortices that were previously obscured [S1]. This representation preserves the full integrity of the original dataset, making it more adaptable to analysis than geometric rendering techniques [S1].
+
+Subsequent developments introduced more sophisticated illumination models. Analytic shading methods, such as Phong shading, became standard for providing specular highlights and diffuse lighting to enhance depth perception in medical imaging [S4]. To address the computational cost of per-sample lighting, illumination caching techniques were developed to pre-compute lighting distributions in 3D textures [S1]. More recently, extinction-based methods using Summed Area Tables (SATs) have been introduced to efficiently approximate soft shadows and ambient occlusion by aggregating extinction coefficients, offering a balance between visual quality and performance [S1]. Next Event Estimation (NEE) has also been adapted for emissive volumes to reduce noise in path-traced scenes [S1].
 
